1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,885
Meet Daniel and Noa, a couple in their
forties with two children.

2
00:00:04,935 --> 00:00:10,502
They want to know when they can retire,
and whether their money will last.

3
00:00:10,552 --> 00:00:14,146
In this video we plan it together, step by
step,

4
00:00:14,196 --> 00:00:17,107
in the Israeli Retirement Planning app.

5
00:00:17,168 --> 00:00:20,988
First, privacy. There is no account and no
server.

6
00:00:21,038 --> 00:00:24,935
Everything you enter stays in this
browser, and the

7
00:00:24,985 --> 00:00:28,418
files you import are read on your own
device.

8
00:00:28,468 --> 00:00:30,275
Let's click Get started.

9
00:00:30,475 --> 00:00:34,027
The fastest way to start is the report
from the

10
00:00:34,077 --> 00:00:37,475
Pension Clearing House, known as the
Mislaka.

11
00:00:37,525 --> 00:00:42,456
One file lists every pension fund,
provident fund and study fund.

12
00:00:42,506 --> 00:00:45,368
Daniel begins by importing his report.

13
00:00:46,070 --> 00:00:49,011
We choose the ZIP file Daniel received.

14
00:00:49,061 --> 00:00:53,228
The app reads it on this device, and
before anything is

15
00:00:53,278 --> 00:00:57,446
saved it shows what it found: the
provider, the balance

16
00:00:57,496 --> 00:01:00,820
and the contribution status of each
account.

17
00:01:00,876 --> 00:01:05,446
We tick that Daniel is married, so the
plan will cover the

18
00:01:05,496 --> 00:01:09,269
whole household, and import the selected
values.

19
00:01:09,326 --> 00:01:13,472
Done. Daniel's accounts are in the plan,
and his age

20
00:01:13,522 --> 00:01:16,862
and salary were filled in from the report.

21
00:01:17,604 --> 00:01:21,393
Now Noa. We open the Partner tab and
import her

22
00:01:21,443 --> 00:01:24,497
Clearing House report in the same way.

23
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:27,760
The app can tell that this report fits the

24
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:31,551
partner, so it saves the accounts under
her name.

25
00:01:31,601 --> 00:01:36,811
We import, and her age and salary arrive
together with the accounts.

26
00:01:37,904 --> 00:01:41,699
Both partners' pension and savings
accounts now

27
00:01:41,749 --> 00:01:44,725
sit in one place, ready for the plan.

28
00:01:45,585 --> 00:01:50,247
A report can't tell us everything, so we
add what is missing.

29
00:01:50,297 --> 00:01:53,954
Daniel wants to retire at 65, and his tax
credit

30
00:01:54,004 --> 00:01:58,048
points, the Nekudot Zikui from his
payslip, are 2.25.

31
00:01:58,099 --> 00:02:02,849
On the Partner tab, Noa plans to retire at
63, and her credit

32
00:02:02,899 --> 00:02:07,492
points are 4.75, which include the points
for the children.

33
00:02:07,546 --> 00:02:12,019
Now the step shows that the required
details are complete.

34
00:02:12,069 --> 00:02:15,372
Credit points lower the income tax, and
the

35
00:02:15,422 --> 00:02:18,725
retirement age sets when withdrawals
begin.

36
00:02:18,775 --> 00:02:24,591
Next, investments. Over the years the
couple bought shares through a broker.

37
00:02:24,641 --> 00:02:28,218
Typing every purchase takes time, so we
use the

38
00:02:28,268 --> 00:02:31,382
CSV option: Add private trade, then Bulk.

39
00:02:31,435 --> 00:02:34,875
Each line holds the purchase date, the
ticker

40
00:02:34,925 --> 00:02:37,589
symbol, the quantity and the price.

41
00:02:37,639 --> 00:02:43,328
We paste the lines, and the app previews
every row before adding anything.

42
00:02:43,392 --> 00:02:46,955
Six purchases of Microsoft, Apple,
Alphabet and

43
00:02:47,005 --> 00:02:50,031
Amazon, worth about 88 thousand dollars.

44
00:02:50,081 --> 00:02:54,182
The app converts them to shekels at the
Bank of Israel

45
00:02:54,232 --> 00:02:57,642
rate and shows the taxable gain on every
lot.

46
00:02:57,694 --> 00:03:02,733
Now bank savings. We open the deposit form
and enter a fixed-term

47
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:07,587
deposit: Bank Hapoalim, for an amount of
150 thousand shekels.

48
00:03:07,644 --> 00:03:12,837
Then the start date, March 2026, the
maturity date, March 2027, the

49
00:03:12,887 --> 00:03:17,533
rate type, fixed-term, and the interest,
4.2 percent a year.

50
00:03:17,583 --> 00:03:18,394
We save it.

51
00:03:21,038 --> 00:03:26,574
A second deposit, this time at Bank Leumi,
for 80 thousand shekels.

52
00:03:28,125 --> 00:03:32,213
This one runs for three years, from
November 2025 to

53
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:36,589
November 2028, at 3.9 percent a year,
again fixed-term.

54
00:03:41,447 --> 00:03:45,297
The app works out each deposit's current
value with

55
00:03:45,347 --> 00:03:48,815
interest, about 237 thousand shekels in
total.

56
00:03:48,865 --> 00:03:52,945
Together with the shares, the couple's
investments and

57
00:03:52,995 --> 00:03:56,768
deposits come to more than half a million
shekels.

58
00:03:56,820 --> 00:04:01,148
Now spending. How much will the couple
need every month?

59
00:04:01,198 --> 00:04:06,856
We choose the itemised list, so that every
expense is counted on its own.

60
00:04:06,919 --> 00:04:10,528
Instead of a blank page we open the
suggestions.

61
00:04:10,578 --> 00:04:16,551
They are grouped by category, and under
each name the app shows how often it is

62
00:04:16,601 --> 00:04:21,811
paid: every month, every two months, once
a year, or every few years.

63
00:04:21,862 --> 00:04:26,045
We tick the first items: Arnona, the
building committee,

64
00:04:26,095 --> 00:04:29,597
electricity, groceries, fuel and car
insurance.

65
00:04:29,647 --> 00:04:34,737
For each one we enter what a single
payment costs, in today's money.

66
00:04:34,787 --> 00:04:40,255
Arnona is paid every two months, so we
enter the amount of one such bill.

67
00:04:40,310 --> 00:04:44,602
Then the bigger and rarer items: replacing
the car, which

68
00:04:44,652 --> 00:04:48,562
happens every seven years, private health
insurance,

69
00:04:48,612 --> 00:04:52,980
restaurants, a yearly trip abroad and help
for the family.

70
00:04:53,030 --> 00:04:56,560
One click adds all eleven expenses to the
list.

71
00:04:59,507 --> 00:05:02,382
Anything missing can be added by hand.

72
00:05:02,432 --> 00:05:05,230
Here we add household help: a name, a

73
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:08,616
category, the amount, and a necessity
level.

74
00:05:08,666 --> 00:05:11,849
We mark it as discretionary, because it is

75
00:05:11,899 --> 00:05:14,543
nice to have rather than essential.

76
00:05:15,598 --> 00:05:19,295
The result: planned spending of about 24
thousand

77
00:05:19,345 --> 00:05:22,813
shekels a month, roughly half of it
essential.

78
00:05:22,863 --> 00:05:27,249
Costs that come only now and then, such as
replacing a car

79
00:05:27,299 --> 00:05:30,919
every seven years, are spread across the
months.

80
00:05:30,982 --> 00:05:33,777
Now the moment of truth: Projections.

81
00:05:33,827 --> 00:05:38,236
Retiring at 65, with planned spending of
about 24 thousand

82
00:05:38,286 --> 00:05:42,080
shekels a month, the plan reads: funded to
age 90.

83
00:05:42,130 --> 00:05:45,232
The couple's money covers their spending.

84
00:05:45,295 --> 00:05:50,817
The chart shows the household's net worth
year by year, in today's money.

85
00:05:50,867 --> 00:05:54,481
The dashed lines mark when each partner
retires.

86
00:05:54,531 --> 00:05:57,992
After that the line dips as they start to
draw

87
00:05:58,042 --> 00:06:01,045
on their savings, and then keeps rising.

88
00:06:04,185 --> 00:06:08,120
With one click we can compare retiring
early, at 60,

89
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:11,338
with retiring at 65, or delaying until 68.

90
00:06:11,388 --> 00:06:15,553
The charts and cards on the page follow
the choice, and

91
00:06:15,603 --> 00:06:19,078
we can always return to the standard
scenario.

92
00:06:21,266 --> 00:06:25,149
Under Income and tax, the app shows the
sustainable

93
00:06:25,199 --> 00:06:28,774
monthly income by source, before and after
tax.

94
00:06:28,824 --> 00:06:32,245
Even in the leanest year the income after
tax

95
00:06:32,295 --> 00:06:34,945
is well above the planned spending.

96
00:06:35,325 --> 00:06:40,169
Spending shows the retirement budget,
split into essential and

97
00:06:40,219 --> 00:06:44,432
discretionary costs, with the irregular
ones included.

98
00:06:44,491 --> 00:06:47,316
Markets never move in a straight line.

99
00:06:47,366 --> 00:06:51,098
The risk analysis replays the plan on two
thousand

100
00:06:51,148 --> 00:06:55,032
random market histories, and the
historical backtest

101
00:06:55,082 --> 00:06:58,966
checks it against 54 real starting years
since 1928.

102
00:06:59,016 --> 00:07:02,446
For this couple the chance that their
spending

103
00:07:02,496 --> 00:07:05,170
lasts to age 90 is about 99 percent.

104
00:07:05,234 --> 00:07:07,036
Finally, Retirement age.

105
00:07:07,086 --> 00:07:10,971
The app finds the earliest feasible
retirement age,

106
00:07:11,021 --> 00:07:14,597
and compares income after tax at 60, 65
and 68.

107
00:07:14,647 --> 00:07:18,841
This couple is on track, and could even
retire earlier.

108
00:07:18,904 --> 00:07:22,277
Your plan is stored only in this browser,
so

109
00:07:22,327 --> 00:07:26,245
download a backup and keep the file
somewhere safe.

110
00:07:26,295 --> 00:07:30,368
You can load it back from Settings
whenever you like.

111
00:07:30,879 --> 00:07:36,072
That is the whole journey: import your
reports, add what is missing,

112
00:07:36,122 --> 00:07:41,393
enter investments, savings and expenses,
and see when you can retire.

113
00:07:41,443 --> 00:07:43,629
Try it with your own numbers.
